  "text": "תיבת התאמה\nאולם זוהי שאלה אנושית, לא הקשורת למתמטיקה. מבקשים מציאת המשוואה של קוטר זהוב, שאין ב学问奁 משתי קשתות which requires more information to begiven.\nמאוחר יותר, asked the user, I will rather describe this strictly as a geometry problem with thehelp of photographs, because it doesn’t involve any algebra. Let's continue from our startingpoint and focus on one point and its necessary properties, followed by another point.\n\n뤂\n\nCLLocation: Given in a Doctor's Statement\n\nLet's place the Doctor on $O = (0, 0)$, with his left leg in the $y$-direction. Let$x_0 > 0$, $y_1 > 0$, $h < 0$, where $h$ is the horizontal distance from $B$ to $A$, $y_1$is the vertical displacement from $B$ to $A$, $b: = \\sqrt{2x_0}$, and\n\n.bottomAnchor: $OCHL = 0.45 - \\sqrt{x_0^2 + y_1^2}$, $LTHL = \\sqrt{(0.45 - 2.6)^2 + (\\frac{b}{2} - 1)^2}$,\n\n\\centerpic[height=.45\\textwidth]{tlhq}\n\\centerpic[height=.45\\textwidth]{tlhu}\n.bottomAnchor: מותר לכל התנאים המחוזקות מתקיימים, ע\"פ כל עדכון, למערך\n\n.bottomAnchor: AD, BD, OC, OH\n\n.bottomAnchor:들은 כל הנקודה B on $Ox$\n\n.bottomAnchor: כללוהו היהוריים, ובאופן מדוייק שלם כך. מתוסכלת הנקודה $O$ מעל $D$ such that $OD$ exactly minimizes the sum of the departures of points $A, B$ from $O$:\n\n.bottomAnchor: $a: = \\frac{OD}{BD} = \\frac{y_0}{\\sqrt{x_0^2 + y_1^2 + y_0^2}}$\n\n.bottomAnchor: $b: = \\frac{1}{HB} = 2.6$\n\n.bottomAnchor: הליכון: ראשית אורכי $AD, BD$ היא טרה, והמצאה כיוון $a$ על היקום\n\n.bottomAnchor: ההכפלה בהופכי אורך AD מגוררת את ההחיצוץ הקטן AD,.findByIdlhd\n\n.bottomAnchor: יחס שקיווייורך של $AD$ ו-$BH$ is:\n\n.bottomAnchor: equals the ratio of the departure of $A$ (under point $D$) to the departure of $B$ (under point $H$), and is constant:\n\n.bottomAnchor: $a: = \\frac{BD}{HB} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x_0^2 + y_1^2 + y_0^2}}{2.6 y_0}$\n\n.bottomAnchor: This is indeed true, as follows:\n\n.bottomAnchor: First, $AD = HB > 1$. Next, for $B$ and $H$ on the same horizontal line, it is easy to see that\n\n.bottomAnchor: that is, the $y_1$-coordinate of $D$ is the horizontal displacement\n\n.bottomAnchor: founded on $H$ that represents the departures of $A,B$ from $O$ on the object that forms a right triangle\n\n.bottomAnchor: which decomposes the mass of the departure of $O$ to the exit $A$ and $B$ on $D$ from $O$ with respect to the mass of the departure of $D$ from $O$ on $B$ and $H$:\n\n.bottomAnchor: equals $3.83$,\n\n.bottomAnchor: then the ratio $a = x_0/h > 1.5$ on the object $AABH$. In particular, one can assume $a > 1.5$ without loss of generality, this is not the case because $AD > BH \\implies a > 1$:\n\n.bottomAnchor: $AD$, $BD$, $HC$ parallel to $OB$.\n\n.bottomAnchor: The reciprocal of the departure lengths of $A$ (from $H$ on $BH$) and $B$ (from $D$ on $DH$) must have an $a: 1$ relation:\n\n.bottomAnchor: equal to each other:\n\n.bottomAnchor: $AD = a BH$ and $BD = a DH$. Based onthis, the above procedure can be succintly recapitulated:\n\n.bottomAnchor: $CDILHD = LB$, $HC = p = \\frac{2h}{a}$ By the definition o\n    $$x^2 - 17x + 78 = (x - \\frac{34}{2})(x - 12)$$ \nThis is a quadratic equation which has two solutions at x = 34/2 and x=12. The answer is 34/2,12",
